group(30, '''
Domain states which inputs the original rule permits, including application restrictions. | Contrast an algebraic denominator exclusion with a meaningful contextual restriction and check a new input. | Restoring a cancelled forbidden input.
For noncircular conics eccentricity can be expressed as focus distance divided by distance to a directrix; ellipse 0<e<1, parabola e=1, hyperbola e>1. | Compare how loci change and explain that a circle has e=0 as the ellipse limiting case, not a finite directrix example. | Claiming e is always c/a for a parabola or giving a circle a finite directrix.
Where a finite derivative exists it is the tangent slope at that point. | Compare secant slopes of x^2 near x=1 with limit 2 and distinguish slope from a tangent angle. | Treating a derivative as the area or every vertical tangent as a finite derivative.
The definite integral is signed accumulation; geometric area uses absolute value where the function changes sign. | Contrast integral of x from -1 to 1, which is 0, with geometric area 1. | Calling a signed integral the positive area in all cases.
The modulus of a+bi is its distance from zero; modulus of z-w is distance between points. | Verify 3+4i has modulus 5 and compare distances from different centers. | Omitting the square root or confusing distance with argument.
Binomial symmetry pairs choosing k with omitting n-k; Pascal's recurrence partitions by one distinguished item. | Explain C(n,k)=C(n,n-k) and C(n,k)=C(n-1,k-1)+C(n-1,k) with boundaries. | Applying the recurrence without valid indices or confusing term coefficients with binomial coefficients.
''')
group(35, '''
Avoidance and negative self-statements are observations, not a diagnosis of inability. | Collect a small work sample, ask privately about the sticking point and distinguish skill gaps from task or confidence barriers. | Labelling a child lazy or diagnosing a disorder from homework refusal.
Homework resistance can have multiple causes including difficulty, access and task meaning. | Ask nonjudgmental questions, compare performance with support and choose a provisional intervention with a check. | Declaring a single psychological cause without evidence.
A small goal should be attainable with support while advancing a real learning objective. | Break a task into a visible first success, feedback and a slightly less supported retry. | Giving meaningless easy work indefinitely or promising immediate confidence recovery.
Feedback should refer to observed strategies and progress. | Provide a specific teacher statement and an achievable next step, then ask what helped. | Empty ability praise or rewards as a guaranteed cure.
Parents can support independence by prompting and gradually reducing help. | Give a brief home routine, a worked supportive prompt and a route to tell the teacher about difficulty. | Requiring parents to complete the assignment or blaming them for all avoidance.
Coordination requires a small shared goal and consistent evidence, with appropriate privacy. | Agree on manageable workload, a common supportive response and a review point across teachers. | Publicly labelling a child or collecting unnecessary sensitive details.
''')
group(43, '''
Upper-primary labor projects should have an age-appropriate purpose and observable skills. | Offer a few feasible options with materials, time and one success criterion each, letting the teacher choose based on resources. | Assuming every school has a garden, workshop or budget.
Safety learning needs supervision and a task-specific risk check before activity. | Choose an age-appropriate planting or simple construction example with safe materials, roles and a stop/help rule. | Handing out unfamiliar hazardous tools without preparation or claiming risk is zero.
Teamwork develops through interdependent contributions and reflection on decisions. | Assign meaningful rotating roles, introduce one solvable design problem and check individual contributions. | Assuming group seating automatically creates collaboration.
Assess process and skill evidence rather than appearance alone. | Give a short observation rubric, a student explanation and before/after evidence of one improvement. | Giving the same group score as proof of every student's mastery.
Integration should preserve an assessable goal in each subject. | Connect a labor task to a measurement/calculation and a clear written/oral explanation with separate checks. | Decorating a practical task with unrelated subject labels.
Reluctance may reflect access, confidence or lack of meaning. | Offer bounded choices, a small supported role and feedback, then ask whether participation improved. | Coercion or assuming unwillingness proves a character defect.
''')
group(50, '''
New second-graders need explicit modeling of a small cooperation routine. | Demonstrate four meaningful roles, rehearse one simple handover and observe before starting the experiment. | Saying cooperate without teaching observable actions.
Shared magnets need equitable turn-taking and roles that all contribute. | Pause grabbing, model passing and rotate operator/observer/recorder/material roles with a participation check. | Permanently assigning operation to the strongest pupil.
Fairness means meaningful roles and predictable rotation, not winning a contest for the preferred role. | Explain each role's value, set a short rotation and offer a supported first turn. | Using the same winner-takes-all game repeatedly as the whole solution.
Listening can be taught as an observable turn-taking routine. | Model a short turn and paraphrase before adding an idea; use the routine to decide one experiment step. | Requiring silence without checking comprehension.
Quiet students need accessible routes into a meaningful contribution. | Offer a prepared partner turn or nonpublic contribution, then gradually increase participation with their input. | Forcing public speech or assigning quiet pupils permanently to recording.
Material handling should be modeled before distribution. | Demonstrate an appropriate thermometer handover, designate a materials role and confirm a stop signal before work. | Distributing fragile equipment before explaining safe handling.
Reporting can distribute evidence across group members. | Let students prepare a short contribution or alternate accessible format and rotate speaking responsibilities. | Cold-calling an unprepared anxious child as the only participation mechanism.
One period cannot support unlimited inquiry and cooperation instruction. | Choose one measurement goal and one cooperation behavior, budget both and include a short debrief. | Omitting all cooperation instruction or claiming every possible objective fits.
Explain the value of collaboration through complementary evidence and checking, not slower work for its own sake. | Give a small task where members compare measurements or explanations and retain individual accountability. | Claiming groups are always faster than individuals.
A short retrieval and rehearsal can reactivate a routine. | Ask students to demonstrate one previous rule, practice it and close with evidence of one improvement. | Repeating a long lecture on every rule without practice.
''')
group(52, '''
A low-resource project can use safe accessible observations with a clear question. | Propose a third-grade campus observation project with a paper record, a feasible product and no required purchases. | Requiring unavailable laboratory equipment or claiming no preparation is needed.
Novices need a bounded question and a modeled first inquiry step. | Demonstrate one observation, let students choose a small question and provide a milestone check. | Giving an undefined project and expecting independent planning immediately.
Records need a purpose and a simple storage routine. | Use dated labelled sheets or a shared folder, capture one change and connect the record to the final explanation. | Collecting large amounts of documentation that are never used.
Forty-five pupils in eleven groups can be ten groups of four and one group of five. | Give four core roles plus a meaningful fifth verifier/timekeeper role and rotate contributions. | Leaving the forty-fifth student without a role or stating eleven equal groups of four.
A campus inquiry needs a bounded comparison and minimal disturbance. | Compare safe observable plant/soil features with repeatable records and a clear question, without leaving campus. | Requiring off-campus travel or destructive collection.
A soil project can be divided into question, safe observation, comparison and explanation. | Provide class-sized milestones with records and a final evidence-based comparison. | Treating visual soil differences as a complete chemical analysis.
Different work rates need an essential checkpoint plus extensions. | Set a minimum evidence target, offer early finishers a deeper comparison and provide a bounded continuation path. | Keeping everyone idle or penalizing all slow groups automatically.
A useful audience or application gives the product a purpose. | Connect a feasible project outcome to a class display, school decision or guide with an accuracy check. | Claiming real-world impact without use or feedback evidence.
Lowering the access barrier should preserve the intellectual goal. | Provide templates, pre-cut safe materials or partner support and gradually fade support. | Removing all student decisions or requiring unpracticed tool use.
A simple process assessment can follow planning, evidence and revision. | Give a three-part checklist with one concrete observation per part and a feedback/retry opportunity. | Scoring only decoration or claiming a checklist proves long-term growth.
''')
group(53, '''
A child can retain agency while receiving a smaller cutting task and a model. | Offer a visible guide and short supported attempt with suitable scissors and supervision, then fade help. | Completing the entire craft for the pupil or withholding help regardless of distress.
Troubleshooting a low-voltage circuit should isolate one possible fault at a time. | Ask the pupil to trace the closed path, inspect contacts and test one component, escalating hints only when needed. | Rewiring everything for the pupil or allowing short circuits.
A collapsed paper bridge provides evidence about the design, not the child's ability. | Acknowledge frustration, compare one structural change and retest under the same load conditions. | Praising an untested improvement as definitely stronger or labelling the child incapable.
Measurement requires a straight aligned scale, a correct starting mark and consistent endpoint reading. | Prompt the child to compare alignments and choose a stabilizing method, then repeat a measurement. | Giving a memorized length without correcting the measurement process.
A simple filter can reduce suspended particles but does not guarantee safe drinking water. | Guide one comparison of flow or layer arrangement and observe clarity without tasting the water. | Claiming clear filtered water is potable.
Magnification needs appropriate object-lens-eye geometry and adjustment. | Calmly model changing one distance and ask the pupil to find the clearest view using a leaf away from direct sun. | Looking at the sun or treating one fixed distance as universally correct.
A veering model car can have alignment, friction or thrust asymmetry. | Record the path, choose one hypothesis and change one factor before a new trial, with a pause if frustrated. | Claiming a specific cause without checking or changing every part simultaneously.
A specimen label needs legible identifying information linked to the right object. | Give a model with specimen ID, known name or unknown label, location and date, then a self-check. | Inventing a rock identification or collection site.
Simplification should preserve the central design idea while reducing construction demands. | Ask the pupil to choose one feature to retain, prototype a simpler structure and explain the tradeoff. | Dismissing the idea or taking over the whole design.
Overflow calls for a calm stop and teacher-managed cleanup before further inquiry. | After safety is restored, compare smaller measured quantities in an open appropriate container and record the change. | Sealing the gas-producing vessel or treating panic as disobedience.
''')
group(54, '''
An objective should specify observable performance and evidence, not merely know. | Refine the bulb goal into a safe low-voltage connection plus an explanation of the closed path, and align a check. | Claiming components named from memory alone demonstrate a working circuit.
Reported recall is a starting point, not evidence of explaining mechanisms or transfer. | Check a new seed example, then extend to explaining how a feature supports dispersal with evidence. | Repeating only names or assuming the entire class needs no further learning.
The goal should match available prerequisites while preserving the important concept. | Shift the core assessment to observable state changes and evidence, using a simple model only if it aids explanation. | Treating an oversimplified molecular story as a directly observed fact.
Two classes can share a core objective while differing in scaffolds and extension. | State a common magnetism criterion and class-specific support/evidence routes based on the described needs. | Permanently lowering expectations for an entire labelled class.
Use the supplied requirement as a premise, not a verified quotation from an unavailable standard. | Deepen observation, comparison and evidence-based description without inventing extra compulsory content. | Claiming the official standard was checked or filling time with unrelated advanced facts.
Engineering objectives include justified design choices, testing and revision, not just producing an object. | Add a measurable temperature comparison under stated conditions and evidence for one revision with supervised use. | Guaranteeing a target temperature under unspecified weather.
Remembering phase order alone does not demonstrate a model of Moon phases. | Use a light-source/Earth/Moon model with a viewpoint explanation and an accessible check. | Explaining ordinary lunar phases as Earth's shadow.
Review goals should target connections and application. | Ask students to classify and justify new examples using evidence, then address identified misconceptions. | Replacing all review with a teacher's list of definitions.
Use the stated vocabulary and experience needs, not family status as a proxy for ability. | Set an observable sound-vibration goal with concrete demonstration, language support and an individual check. | Assuming left-behind children cannot understand science.
The science objective should concern observable plant characteristics and evidence-based comparison. | Align diaries and drawings with accurate observation records and a science-specific criterion. | Grading artistic skill as the principal evidence of scientific learning.
''')
group(55, '''
Lab routines need a small number of observable actions taught before materials are active. | Model a start/stop/handling routine, rehearse briefly and check understanding before proceeding. | Expecting a verbal list alone to establish routines.
Attention during instructions depends on material access and clear sequence. | Pause handling, model the next action and ask a pupil to demonstrate before reopening materials. | Shouting over ongoing unsafe activity or punishing every distracted pupil.
Work-time variation needs essential checkpoints and useful extensions. | Give a visible timebox, check a stalled group and offer finishers an evidence-based extension. | Assuming every slow pupil is deliberately wasting time.
Distribution should use designated roles and a rehearsed handover. | Prepare small sets, call one materials person per group and stop pushing before continuing. | Distributing materials into a scramble or using collective humiliation.
A stop cue must be understood and practiced, with accessible forms. | Rehearse a brief stop-and-hands-clear action, check who missed it and reteach accordingly. | Treating a conditioned response as automatic or imposing escalating punishment without checking access.
Consistency can reduce confusion when teachers agree on meanings. | Confirm the existing cue, retain its action and explain any science-specific addition. | Using the same cue for contradictory actions.
Young children can learn rules through concrete modeling and practice. | Act out a correct and safely simulated incorrect handover, then let pupils demonstrate the expected action. | Demonstrating an actually dangerous act to make the point.
A temporary material pause can address immediate risk while retaining a route back to participation. | Explain the specific behavior, reteach and allow a supported retry after the situation is safe. | Permanent exclusion or confiscation as humiliation.
The end of a lesson needs safe closure and a minimum evidence checkpoint. | Stop in time to store materials, record current observations and plan continuation where feasible. | Keeping hazardous work running past supervision or declaring unfinished work worthless.
Different classroom behavior needs investigation of routines and task demands. | Observe transitions, coordinate cues with the homeroom teacher and test one change before generalizing. | Concluding the science teacher or pupils are inherently incapable.
''')
group(56, '''
Magnets do not attract all metals or all materials. | Sequence predictions, observations on named materials, explanation and a transfer question with contingent follow-ups. | Teaching that every metal is magnetic.
A correct statement can be opened for evidence and peer reasoning. | Ask classmates to identify the observed evidence, compare an alternative explanation and extend the answer respectfully. | Only saying correct or requiring peers to repeat the same sentence.
Leaf observations can be organized by a shared dimension without erasing varied observations. | Focus on one feature, compare two examples and then broaden with a reasoned question. | Treating all unexpected observations as wrong.
Silence may reflect preparation or participation barriers. | Give thinking/pair time, ask for one observation or question about the report and invite a low-pressure response. | Forcing immediate public answers or assuming no learning occurred.
A controlled pendulum comparison changes one variable while keeping effective length and release amplitude appropriate. | Progress from what changed to what stayed fixed, repeated timing and an evidence-based conclusion. | Inferring mass causes period change from an uncontrolled trial.
Engineering questions can ask about criteria, tradeoffs and evidence from testing. | Ask students to predict how a lens-position change affects the image, test it and justify a revision. | Equating assembly completion with understanding or directing viewing of the sun.
Different participation patterns need varied response routes but a shared scientific goal. | Use the same sound-vibration question with quiet thinking/writing or exploratory extensions and check understanding in both classes. | Assuming talkativeness measures scientific ability.
An incorrect answer is evidence for a follow-up, not a reason for personal criticism. | Ask for reasoning, supply a counterexample or observation and invite a revised explanation. | Endorsing the incorrect science to protect feelings or publicly humiliating the student.
Physical versus chemical change concerns whether new substances form, not reversibility alone. | Compare evidence in a new example and ask what additional observation would support a claim. | Using irreversible as a sufficient definition of chemical change.
Unexpected answers should be checked for relevance and underlying reasoning. | Follow one productive idea with evidence questions, correct misconceptions and return to the core objective when needed. | Forcing every answer back to the guide's exact wording.
''')
group(57, '''
A quick role conflict needs a fair temporary procedure and a later turn for each pupil. | Use a brief rotation or agreed selection, model role duties and resume with a check. | Automatically failing any temporary teacher assignment or allowing one permanent leader.
Reluctance to lead may reflect unclear duties or pressure. | Clarify a small supported leader task, offer co-leading or a temporary scaffold and rotate later. | Forcing one unwilling pupil into public leadership without support.
Monopolizing equipment requires an immediate clear boundary with respectful feedback. | Pause the handover, specify turns and give the pupil a constructive role while verifying others' access. | Avoiding all intervention to prevent public criticism or labelling the pupil as bad.
A quiet pupil needs a meaningful accessible choice, not a permanent peripheral role. | Offer a prepared or partner-supported contribution and check privately what helps. | Always assigning quiet children to recording or forcing a public performance.
Turn-taking is learned through a visible simple sequence. | Model passing an object, rehearse short turns and use a visual cue with feedback. | Explaining only abstract fairness to first-graders.
Agreed random selection can be one tool when roles are safe and equitable over time. | Combine it with clear duties, rotation and teacher intervention if exclusion or access barriers appear. | Treating any student vote or lottery as automatically fair.
Exclusion needs teacher action based on observed behavior and a protected route to participation. | Check the situation, stop exclusion, assign a meaningful supported contribution and follow up privately. | Leaving the targeted student without a role to preserve group autonomy.
A pupil with weaker practical skills needs scaffolded access to real work. | Choose a manageable operation, model or partner-support it and recognize observed progress. | Permanently excluding the pupil from operation or fabricating success.
Roles should divide real work and rotate, with workload checked. | Define concrete responsibilities and one handover rule, then check every member's contribution. | Fixed personality-based roles that make some children permanent bystanders.
Teacher-assigned roles can be a temporary scaffold that is gradually reduced. | Move from modeled assignment to guided rotation and student negotiation with review criteria. | Claiming all direct assignment is inherently prohibited.
''')
group(60, '''
A missing alcohol lamp does not justify improvising an unsafe heat source. | Use a suitable supervised demonstration if equipment exists, otherwise a video plus safe observations and explicit limits on what was observed directly. | Requiring children to heat water unsupervised or rejecting every video alternative.
Different materials can distract from the target shape comparison. | Use matched sets or an explicit comparison rule, teach a handover and check recognition independently of color. | Scoring color preference as shape understanding.
Weak bubbling does not prove the baking soda expired. | Acknowledge the observation, check materials and setup later, and use a labelled recorded demonstration or another safe evidence source for the lesson. | Inventing a definite cause or fabricating experimental results.
Unsafe material play needs an immediate calm pause and a retaught routine. | Stop access, model one handling action and return materials after a supported practice. | Continuing instructions while objects are thrown or using humiliation.
Collection needs planned time, labelled places and assigned responsibilities. | Model a count-and-return routine; adults handle broken glass under school procedures. | Asking pupils to pick up broken glass by hand or guaranteeing cleanup takes a fixed number of seconds.
A substitute elastic device needs calibration to measure force quantitatively. | Distinguish qualitative extension comparison from a calibrated force measure and give an appropriate low-risk classroom alternative. | Treating uncalibrated stretch as an accurate force reading in newtons.
Equitable access requires meaningful rotating roles and a check of participation. | Set operator, observer, recorder and materials duties, rotate at a sensible checkpoint and support novice handling. | Giving all operation to the quickest pupil.
Sharp rock preparation is not made safe merely by careful distribution. | Replace pupil cutting with pre-prepared specimens or a suitable supervised demonstration; retain observation and recording goals. | Giving ordinary primary pupils knives and glass for unsupervised rock cutting.
Bring-from-home materials need a clear permitted list and a school-provided fallback. | Specify safe simple items, ask pupils not to bring unknown hazardous objects and check before use. | Penalizing pupils without supplies or accepting every found material.
Different classes may need different rehearsal and distribution scaffolds. | Observe one bottleneck, adjust a role or cue and compare the next transition rather than blaming the class. | Assuming identical instructions must produce identical behavior.
''')
